<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Upper Canada</h1><p class="st-meta">1791 CE – 1841 CE · capital York</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The English-speaking British colony founded for Loyalists north of the Great Lakes, forerunner of the province of Ontario.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-act-1791">Constitutional Act</a> of 1791 split the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-quebec">Province of Quebec</a>, creating Upper <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-canada">Canada</a> upriver from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> for the Loyalists who had settled there after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>. Its first lieutenant governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-graves-simcoe">John Graves Simcoe</a>, laid out roads and moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niagara-on-the-lake">Newark</a> to York, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a>, in 1796.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Upper <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-canada">Canada</a> had English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a>, freehold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">land tenure</a> and an elected assembly, but power lay with the lieutenant governor and an appointed elite known as the Family Compact. Land set aside as clergy reserves for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> was a long-standing grievance. The colony repulsed American invasions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a>, and British immigration greatly increased its population after 1815.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Reformers led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-lyon-mackenzie">William Lyon Mackenzie</a> rose in a brief rebellion in 1837. Following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-lambton-1st-earl-of-durham">Lord Durham's</a> report, Upper <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-canada">Canada</a> was merged with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lower-canada">Lower Canada</a> in 1841 as the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>; it re-emerged as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> at Confederation in 1867.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/upper-canada.jpg" alt="Upper Canada" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Original: Acts of Union 1800 Vector: Yaddah · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_Kingdom_(3-5).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1791 CE – 1841 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>York</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John Graves Simcoe, Francis Bond Head</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism, Methodism, Presbyterianism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Province of Upper Canada</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1816&amp;focus=upper-canada" aria-label="Upper Canada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2772 630 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Upper Canada lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3092 886L3377 637M3092 886L4997 651M3092 886L4997 651M3092 886L2907 423"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3377 637h0M4997 651h0M4997 651h0M2907 423h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3092 886h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3092 886h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks York. <a href="/atlas?year=1816&amp;focus=upper-canada">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
